# Line endings, made explicit because week 15 spent an hour on one.
#
# A shell script whose lines end `\` + CRLF does not continue — the backslash escapes the
# carriage return, and `ash` reports "syntax error: unexpected word (expecting do)" three
# lines away from anything that looks wrong. That was a self-inflicted extraction bug when
# it happened, but the same shape reaches a container for real through a Dockerfile `RUN`
# continuation or a mounted `.sh`, and it is the kind of failure that only appears on
# somebody else's machine.
#
# YAML is exempt from the worry rather than from the rule: the spec normalises line breaks
# inside scalars, so a CRLF compose file yields LF strings. It is listed anyway so the
# repository has one answer instead of two.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Files an interpreter inside a Linux container reads directly. These must be LF on disk,
# not merely LF in the object store.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.hcl           text eol=lf
*.ctmpl         text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
Makefile        text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.sql           text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf

# The rendered templates carry no trailing newline on purpose: Postgres and MinIO read the
# file verbatim, so a newline becomes part of the password and the failure looks like a
# wrong password rather than a wrong file. Nothing here should ever add one.
infra/vault-agent/templates/* -text

*.pdf           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.traineddata   binary
